> 
> > I'm runnning RedHAt 4.2 Linux on DEC PWS 433a(Miata) and having a
> > problem with its clock. The system clock (displayed by 'date' command)
> > goes too faster than the CMOS clock.
> > Andreas Ulrich Trottmann (atrottma@g26.ethz.ch) wrote on
> > Wed, 30 Apr 1997 00:31:43 +0200 (MET DST):
> > > Second: The clock runs quite fast -- after one "real" hour, the system
> > > time has advanced about one hour and ten minutes. The CMOS time reported
> > > by clock -Au -r is correct, however.
> >
> > Does anybody know how to solve this problem?
> 
> I suppose that it is a clock/interrupts per sec is too much -> clock freq is
> higher than the linux/pws implimentors though it was ( by the account some 
> 16% higher ( 1/6 hour ) )
> 
> from a cursorey look at the kern, it diesnt seem to be set there, maybe the
> bootstrap pgm (MILO maybe ????)

Yes, well, actually, the problem *will* be fixed by a new MILO, but it's not
ready yet.

Seems that you have to use extra caution with some of the propagation delays
through the core logic chipset on MIATA (and LX164, for that matter); if you
don't, its possible to take a second interrupt from the timer before the
previous interrupt clear has made its way through the circuitry, thus resulting
in counting some ticks more than once... :-(

A problem with a known solution, but at the moment we can't build the PALcode
(where the fix is) from source under LINUX, so until we *can*, you'll have to
live with this problem.

I promise an announcement on axp-list when the MILO is ready.

--Jay++

